# MCP tool request-path families that no Sorcha service currently maps.
#
# Each line is a route family as reduced by scripts/check-mcp-routes.ps1: query string dropped,
# every route parameter collapsed to '*', lower-cased. Comments start with '#'.
#
# Families are matched PER OWNING SERVICE — the service the tool actually addresses, derived from
# the SorchaService.<X> its typed client or endpoint field resolves. A same-named route in another
# service does not satisfy a call, so each reason below names the service that is missing it.
#
# RATCHET: this list may ONLY shrink. A stale entry (a family that is now mapped, or no longer
# called by any registered tool) FAILS the gate, so an entry must be deleted in the same PR that
# fixes or removes the call. Never add a line here to make a build pass — repoint the tool at the
# route that exists, or map the route in the service.
#
# Seeded 2026-09-05 from the first run of the gate on feature/mcp-agent-experience. These were the
# known-broken calls the gate was written to prove: ten tools were reaching routes that were never
# mapped, including the ENTIRE participant discovery loop, so an agent could submit an action but
# never discover or inspect one.
#
# Task 4 repointed sorcha_inbox_list, sorcha_action_details, sorcha_workflow_instances,
# sorcha_workflow_status, and sorcha_action_validate at routes that actually exist
# (api/actions/pending, api/instances/, api/instances/{id}/actions/{id}, api/instances/{id}, and
# api/execution/validate respectively) and removed their four entries. sorcha_action_validate's
# target was NOT visible to this gate at all — Blueprint Service also maps
# api/actions/{wallet}/{register}, a same-shaped four-segment family, so the static family match
# hid the miss entirely; it was fixed on the task brief's authority, not this gate's, and stayed
# broken through a fully green gate run until then.
#
# Task 5 repointed the remaining four — sorcha_register_query (api/registers/{id}/transactions),
# sorcha_user_list and sorcha_user_manage (api/organizations/{organizationId}/users and its
# suspend/reactivate/unlock/role sub-routes — both tools gained a required organizationId
# parameter the routes need in the path), and sorcha_token_revoke
# (api/auth/token/revoke-user / revoke-organization) — and withdrew sorcha_blueprint_diff from
# the served surface (no /diff endpoint exists anywhere to back it; see BlueprintDiffTool and
# issue #1607). Withdrawing [McpServerToolType] from BlueprintDiffTool removes it from this gate's
# scan entirely, which is why its former api/blueprints/*/diff entry is deleted here rather than
# kept — a family no registered tool calls is stale, and the gate fails stale entries by design.
#
# This list is now EMPTY: every MCP tool's route is mapped by the service it addresses. A future
# regression re-adds an entry only as a last resort, and only alongside a task that will remove it
# again — this ratchet does not reopen just because a new tool ships broken.
